      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "©2012, Wiley Periodicals, Inc.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
            "content": "The purpose of this study was to assess the feasibility and safety of thrombus-aspiration through a 5 Fr guiding catheter with transradial approach in acute coronary syndromes."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "The use of thrombus-aspirating devices improves myocardial reperfusion but requires at least a 6 Fr guiding catheter. Transradial coronary interventions using a 5 Fr guiding catheter are attractive to reduce bleeding complications."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "We retrospectively selected patients presenting acute coronary syndromes with angiographically visible thrombus who underwent thrombus-aspiration through radial access using a 4 Fr multipurpose catheter in a 5 Fr guiding catheter. We described clinical and angiographic characteristics of the cohort, and the procedure's technique, success and complications."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Among the 34 included patients, 29 presented ST-segment elevation myocardial infarction. Complete resolution of the ST-segment elevation was effective in 93% of these patients. TIMI flow grade after thrombus-aspiration was significantly improved compared to baseline TIMI flow grade or after passage of the guidewire (P < 0.001 for both). There was no dissection or perforation but we noted distal embolization in 5 cases probably explained by the high grade of intracoronary thrombus in our cohort."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "Thrombus-aspiration through a 5 Fr guiding catheter with transradial approach seems to be safe and effective in selected patients with acute coronary syndrome. This mini-invasive approach brought the advantages of the transradial access in 5 Fr but also its limitations."
          }
        ]
      },
